description:
## Context

`forge` is a Go CLI that scaffolds repositories from embedded assets. What a generated repo receives comes from three layers, applied in this order (`internal/scaffold/writer.go`):

1. `templates/common/` - shared assets for every stack.
2. `templates/golden/<stack>/` - the "vanilla" layer captured from an upstream generator.
3. `templates/golden/<stack>/.forge-overlay/` - forge's vetted layer. It wins on any path collision.

Version values are literal text in these files. Nothing injects them from Go code. Files ending in `.tmpl` are rendered and lose the suffix. For the TypeScript stacks the overlay `package.json.tmpl` replaces the vanilla one, so the overlay file is the one that ships. No lockfiles ship (ADR-0014), so a range resolves to whatever the registry serves on the day of `forge init`; that is why this epic pins exact versions.

Background reading if a term is unclear: `docs/SPEC.md` section 15, `docs/adr/0005-*`, `docs/adr/0014-*`, `docs/adr/0020-*`, `CONTEXT.md`.

Versions in this ticket were looked up on 2026-11-18 from the upstream registries.

## Goal

OpenTelemetry JS 3 removed `@opentelemetry/sdk-trace-web`. The three TypeScript stacks build their tracer on it, so this is a code migration, not only a version bump. Every decision is already made below; follow it exactly.

Source for everything here: the official migration guide `https://raw.githubusercontent.com/open-telemetry/opentelemetry-js/v3.1.1/doc/3.x/migration-guide.md` and each package's `src/index.ts` at tag `v3.1.1` / `experimental/v0.301.2`.

## Pins

In the overlay `package.json.tmpl` of `vite-ts`, `sveltekit`, `angular` (keep the block alphabetical):

| Package | Now | New |
|---|---|---|
| `@opentelemetry/api` | `1.9.1` | `1.10.0` |
| `@opentelemetry/core` | absent | add at `3.1.1` |
| `@opentelemetry/exporter-metrics-otlp-http`, `exporter-trace-otlp-http`, `instrumentation`, `instrumentation-fetch` | `0.223.2` | `0.301.2` |
| `@opentelemetry/resources`, `sdk-metrics` | `2.12.1` | `3.1.1` |
| `@opentelemetry/sdk-trace` | absent | add at `3.1.1` |
| `@opentelemetry/sdk-trace-web` | `2.12.1` | remove |
| `@opentelemetry/semantic-conventions` | `1.45.0` | unchanged |

`@opentelemetry/core` is needed because the W3C propagators are exported only from it. Do not add any other package.

## Files

- `templates/golden/vite-ts/.forge-overlay/src/lib/telemetry.ts.tmpl` and `telemetry.test.ts`
- `templates/golden/sveltekit/.forge-overlay/src/lib/telemetry.ts.tmpl` and `telemetry.test.ts`
- `templates/golden/angular/.forge-overlay/src/app/telemetry.ts.tmpl` and `telemetry.spec.ts`
- the three overlay `package.json.tmpl`

The vite-ts and sveltekit bootstrap files are byte-identical today, and so are their test files. They must be byte-identical after this change. The Angular bootstrap differs only in how it reads its endpoint and service name (no OTel difference), so it gets the same edits at its own line numbers.

## Change 1: imports

In each `telemetry.ts.tmpl` (vite-ts and sveltekit lines 10-25, angular lines 8-23), replace the import of `@opentelemetry/sdk-trace-web` and add the `core` import, so the OpenTelemetry imports read:

```ts
import { context, metrics, propagation, trace } from '@opentelemetry/api';
import type { Meter, Tracer } from '@opentelemetry/api';
import {
  CompositePropagator,
  W3CBaggagePropagator,
  W3CTraceContextPropagator,
} from '@opentelemetry/core';
import { OTLPMetricExporter } from '@opentelemetry/exporter-metrics-otlp-http';
import { OTLPTraceExporter } from '@opentelemetry/exporter-trace-otlp-http';
import { registerInstrumentations } from '@opentelemetry/instrumentation';
import { FetchInstrumentation } from '@opentelemetry/instrumentation-fetch';
import { resourceFromAttributes } from '@opentelemetry/resources';
import { MeterProvider, PeriodicExportingMetricReader } from '@opentelemetry/sdk-metrics';
import type { IMetricReader } from '@opentelemetry/sdk-metrics';
import {
  BatchSpanProcessor,
  SimpleSpanProcessor,
  StackContextManager,
  TracerProvider,
} from '@opentelemetry/sdk-trace';
import type { SpanExporter, SpanProcessor } from '@opentelemetry/sdk-trace';
import { ATTR_SERVICE_NAME } from '@opentelemetry/semantic-conventions';
```

If the file already imports a name from `@opentelemetry/api` that is not listed here, keep it.

## Change 2: span processors take an options object

In 3.x both processors take `{ exporter }` instead of the exporter itself (vite-ts and sveltekit lines 76-83, angular lines 66-73):

```ts
  const spanProcessors: SpanProcessor[] = [];
  if (options.spanExporter) {
    spanProcessors.push(new SimpleSpanProcessor({ exporter: options.spanExporter }));
  } else if (endpoint) {
    spanProcessors.push(
      new BatchSpanProcessor({
        exporter: new OTLPTraceExporter({ url: `${endpoint}/v1/traces` }),
      }),
    );
  }
```

## Change 3: provider and global registration

`WebTracerProvider` becomes `TracerProvider`, which has no `register()`. Replace the provider construction and the `register()` call (vite-ts and sveltekit lines 96-104, angular lines 86-94) with:

```ts
  const tracerProvider = new TracerProvider({ resource, spanProcessors });
  const meterProvider = new MeterProvider({ resource, readers });

  const registered = options.register ?? true;
  if (registered) {
    // Replaces WebTracerProvider.register(): global tracer provider, W3C trace-context
    // and baggage propagators, and a stack context manager.
    trace.setGlobalTracerProvider(tracerProvider);
    propagation.setGlobalPropagator(
      new CompositePropagator({
        propagators: [new W3CTraceContextPropagator(), new W3CBaggagePropagator()],
      }),
    );
    context.setGlobalContextManager(new StackContextManager().enable());
    metrics.setGlobalMeterProvider(meterProvider);
  }
```

This is what `register()` did internally in 2.x, so behaviour is unchanged. Keep the existing variable names if they differ from this snippet; change only the calls.

Two existing comments mention `register()` (vite-ts line 101 and lines 121-122). Reword them to say "the globals registered above". Leave `shutdown()` and its four `disable()` calls as they are; all four still exist.

## What does not change

`OTLPMetricExporter({ url })`, `PeriodicExportingMetricReader({ exporter })`, `registerInstrumentations({ instrumentations, tracerProvider, meterProvider })`, `new FetchInstrumentation()`, `resourceFromAttributes`, `ATTR_SERVICE_NAME`, and the exported names and signatures of the bootstrap file.

## Change 4: tests

In each of the three test files, line 7 becomes:

```ts
import { InMemorySpanExporter } from '@opentelemetry/sdk-trace';
```

Nothing else in the tests changes. No assertion depends on behaviour that changed: `SimpleSpanProcessor` still exports synchronously for a resource built with `resourceFromAttributes`.

## Decisions already made (do not revisit)

- Use `StackContextManager`, as 2.x did. Do not add `@opentelemetry/context-zone`.
- Keep `@opentelemetry/instrumentation-fetch`. Upstream marks it deprecated in favour of a separate browser package; moving to that is tracked in the backlog ticket, not here.
- Follow the registration order shown above.

## Rules

1. Change only the files listed under "Files". If another file must change for the gate to pass, stop and report it in this ticket.
2. Use exactly the versions listed. Do not pick a newer one, even if one has been released since.
3. Write exact pins: no `^`, `~`, `>=`, `~=`, `latest`, or major-only tags. GitHub Actions use the full commit SHA plus a `# vX.Y.Z` comment.
4. Do not run `forge update`; it would drop hand-maintained manifest lines. Edit manifests by hand.
5. Do not add lockfiles, Renovate/Dependabot, a central versions file, helper scripts, or new tests. Only update existing assertions.
6. Do not refactor, rename, reformat, or remove unused dependencies.
7. Update a test only where it asserts a version string this ticket changes.
8. For a "Verify first" step, run the stated command and use its result. If the result contradicts this ticket, stop and report.
9. If a gate fails for a reason this ticket does not predict, stop and report with the output. Do not skip tests or loosen assertions.
10. If a scenario needs a tool your environment lacks (docker, dotnet, node), say which scenario you could not run. Do not report it as passed.
11. Done means: every scenario under acceptance criteria passes, the change is committed and pushed, and this ticket is closed with `bd close`.
